From the joint probabilities given in Table 5, it becomes clear that coin V is fair, while coin U is unfair--the event 0 happens with probability 5/20 and the event 1 with probability 15/20. The same arguments as in the fair-coin example show that the minimal \(E|U-V|\) and the maximal \(E|U-V|\) are achieved at the joint distributions given in Tables 6 and 7. The minimal \(E|U-V|\) equals 1/4. It cannot equal zero because the one-dimensional distributions are different.

